here, and what neither of them reaches.


WHY TWO LICENCES
================

Three things had to be true at once, and no single licence does all three.

  1. COMMERCIAL USE STAYS PERMITTED.  Neither licence has a non-commercial
     clause.  MixOS may be sold, shipped inside a product, put on a card that
     comes with a handheld somebody paid for, and built into a device offered
     for money.  That was true under the previous licence and it stays true.

  2. THE SOURCE STAYS UNDER CONTROL.  MPL-2.0 is file-level copyleft: change a
     MixOS file and the changed file goes back out under MPL-2.0, so fixes to
     this port return to it.  MPL-2.0 section 3.3 then explicitly permits
     combining those files with proprietary code in a Larger Work, so a vendor's
     own application is not conscripted by living on the same card, in the same
     image, or in the same binary.

  3. IT HAS TO BE COMBINABLE WITH LINUX.  This is what forced the change.  The
     Microsoft Public License is not GPL-compatible -- its section 3(D) imposes
     a condition GPLv2 section 6 forbids adding -- so under Ms-PL none of this
     code could ever be moved into, linked with, or derived from the kernel it
     exists to drive.  MPL-2.0 is written for precisely this problem: sections
     1.12 and 3.3 name the GPL family as SECONDARY LICENSES, so MPL-2.0 code may
     be redistributed under the GPL when it is combined with GPL code.  Offering
     GPL-2.0-or-later outright alongside it removes the last of the ambiguity,
     and it is the licence the kernel modules in linux/ already carry.


SUPERSEDED LICENCE
==================

This work was previously offered under the Microsoft Public License.  Copies
already obtained under Ms-PL stay under Ms-PL -- that grant is not revoked and
cannot be.  From this revision forward the terms are the dual MPL-2.0 /
GPL-2.0-or-later grant above, and the `SPDX-License-Identifier: MS-PL' headers
that used to be at the top of these files now read
`SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-or-later'.


WHAT THE DUAL LICENCE COVERS HERE, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
=======================================================

This directory is not licensed uniformly, and saying otherwise would be a false
statement about other people's code.  A device port is a small amount of new work
threaded through a large amount of existing work, and the existing work keeps its
own terms.

MPL-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-or-later, the dual grant above:

    build-in-vm.sh              the incremental bring-up builder
    generate_dts.py             the DTS generator
    create_boot_image.py        the MT6592 Android boot.img writer
    tools/j36-eglprobe.c        the EGL/GBM/DRM scanout probe
    tools/j36-mixmirror.c       the panel-to-USB-HDMI mirror
    tools/mfgpower.c            the MFG power-domain bring-up probe
    tools/mixsplash.c           the framebuffer boot splash
    tools/mixdash/              the Qt dashboard
    README.md, this file        the documentation

    Each of those carries an `SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0 OR
    GPL-2.0-or-later' header.

GPL-2.0-only, and deliberately NOT part of the dual grant:

    linux/j36_mt6592_input.c    keypad, GPIO keys and the analog sticks
    linux/j36_mt6592_audio.c    MT6592 AFE ASoC card
    linux/j36_mt6592_usb_phy.c  the USB PHY and its VBUS/charger interlock
    linux/j36_mt6592_pmic.c     battery gauge, charger and poweroff
    linux/j36_mt6592_backlight.c   the BLS block behind the panel
    linux/j36_mt6592_wifi.c     the CONSYS connectivity MCU bring-up
    linux/j36_jd9365_panel.c    the JD9365 panel that adopts LK's state
    linux/0001-mtk-sd-mt6592.patch
    linux/0002-drm-mediatek-mt6592.patch

    These are Linux kernel modules and Linux kernel patches.  They carry
    `SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only' and `MODULE_LICENSE("GPL v2")', they
    are derivative of and link against GPL-2.0-only kernel internals, and the two
    patches are diffs against GPL-2.0-only files.  GPL-2.0-ONLY is narrower than
    the GPL-2.0-or-later half of the grant above and narrower than MPL-2.0: these
    files are not dual-licensed, not relicensable, and this project is not free
    to make them so.  Send changes to them upstream; they are GPL either way.

    Note which way the compatibility runs.  Code may move from the dual-licensed
    list into these files, because MPL-2.0 permits redistribution under a
    Secondary Licence and the GPL-2.0-or-later option covers it outright.  Code
    may not move the other way.

Vendor material, redistributed unmodified for provenance:

    mvii-board/                 five verbatim MediaTek/MVII board headers and
                                driver sources, listed with a SHA-256 each in
                                mvii-board/PROVENANCE.txt.  They are inputs the
                                DTS generator parses, not MixOS work, and they
                                carry whatever terms their authors set.

Nothing in this file changes the licence of anything a build downloads or stages:
the Linux kernel, Mesa, Qt, SDL, busybox and the Debian rootfs are each obtained
under their own terms and are not covered here.  A card built
by `build-in-vm.sh' is an aggregate of many licences; this one applies to the
MixOS scripts, drivers, probes and dashboard that assembled it.

A device that boots this card is distributing the Linux kernel, so it is
distributing GPL-2.0 software, and its recipients are owed the corresponding
source: this repository, plus the upstream Linux tree and version that
build-in-vm.sh names.  That obligation is Linux's and Debian's and predates any
choice made here.



THE TWO TEXTS
=============

    LICENSE.MPL-2.0   Mozilla Public License 2.0, verbatim, with both exhibits.
                      https://www.mozilla.org/MPL/2.0/
    LICENSE.GPL-2     GNU General Public License version 2, verbatim.
                      https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/gpl-2.0.html
                      A running MixOS device also carries Debian's copy at
                      /usr/share/common-licenses/GPL-2 .

"Version 2 or any later version" is meant as the FSF writes it: you may instead
follow the terms of any later version published by the Free Software Foundation.

Exhibit B of the MPL is the "Incompatible With Secondary Licenses" notice, and
MixOS does NOT use it.  No file here carries that notice, so MPL section 3.3 is
in force and this code may be redistributed under a Secondary Licence -- the
GPL -- when it is combined with GPL code.  That is deliberate, and it is the
whole reason this licence was chosen.  Do not add Exhibit B to a MixOS file.


ATTRIBUTION
===========

MixOS is a Debian-based operating system for MediaTek and legacy Rockchip
handhelds.  Its build scripts began as dArkOS, itself a Debian-based
continuation of ArkOS; nothing MixOS runs comes from either any more, but the
pipeline that assembles the image does, and it keeps their copyright and their
MIT licence.  Neither project asked for this port, neither endorses it, and
neither is responsible for it.  Full attribution and thanks are in
../../LICENSES.md and ../../README.md at the top of this repository; the short
form is:

  * Debian -- the operating system underneath all of it.  The rootfs a MixOS card
    boots is Debian, built with Debian's own tools, and every package outside
    this directory that is not the kernel came from Debian.  https://www.debian.org
  * The Linux kernel -- the reason there is a device to port to, and the licence
    the modules in linux/ are under.
  * ArkOS, by christianhaitian -- the original handheld distribution, and the
    ancestor of the build pipeline.
  * dArkOS -- the Debian rebuild of ArkOS this repository was forked from, and
    the origin of the image-assembly scripts outside device/j36-ultra/ and
    device/r36-ultra/.
  * MediaTek -- the MT6592 register-level documentation and the vendor driver
    sources this port reads, via the MVII tree in mvii-board/.
  * Mesa (lima and kmsro), Qt, SDL, busybox, doomgeneric and Freedoom, without
    any of which there would be no port.

Neither half of the dual grant conveys any right in a trademark -- MPL-2.0
section 2.3 says so outright, and the GPL has never granted one.  Every trademark
named here belongs to its respective owner.  MixOS is not affiliated with or
endorsed by the Debian project, MediaTek Inc., Rockchip, the Mozilla Foundation or
the Qt Company.
